@inject ApiMetadata Api

@* FilterForm's Overview, written by hand after Table's and the selection Overviews: the three
   filter fields (previewed in each mode), dates, filtering on demand, with a table, your own inputs,
   saying how many match, and its own words. Parts, Accessibility and See also come from the
   metadata and the doc comments; See also adds the order list demo. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A filter form is a GET form: its values go in the address, so a filtered view can be bookmarked, shared and reloaded, and it works with JavaScript off. It doesn't filter anything itself; the page reads the values with <Code>[SupplyParameterFromQuery]</Code> and filters its own data. To filter a table by its columns instead, use the <Anchor Href="docs/components/table">Table</Anchor>'s own column filters.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Search, select and checkbox</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>FilterSearch</Code> for a search box (<Code>?q=</Code> unless you give it a <Code>Name</Code>), <Code>FilterSelect</Code> for a choice with "All" first, and <Code>FilterCheckbox</Code> for a filter that's on or off (<Code>?mine=true</Code>).</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="filter-form-issues" />
<TextBlock>With the script, the search applies after a pause in typing and the others as they change, without a reload, and focus stays where it was. A <Code>FilterSelect</Code>'s options come from <Code>Items</Code> or an enum (<Code>EnumType</Code>), and the URL holds the value, so use <Code>ItemText</Code> when people should see something else.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Dates</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>FilterDate</Code> for a date, or a date and time with <Code>Type="DateFieldType.DateTime"</Code>; the page reads it as a <Code>DateOnly?</Code> or <Code>DateTime?</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="filter-form-dates" />
<TextBlock>The address holds what the browser posts (<Code>2027-03-01</Code>, or <Code>2027-03-01T14:30</Code>), with no time zone, so the page decides which zone a time is in; say it in the <Code>Label</Code>, as in "From (UTC)". <Code>Min</Code> and <Code>Max</Code> limit the dates, as To's <Code>Min</Code> follows From. A date applies a moment after it changes, so typing one part by part applies once.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Filtering on demand</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Live="false"</Code> to keep the Apply button with the script too, so nothing applies until it's pressed or Enter is, and <Code>ApplyText</Code> to rename it.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="filter-form-apply-button" />
<TextBlock>Choose this when each filter is slow to apply, such as a search against a large database, or when people set several filters before looking at the results.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">With a table</Heading>
<TextBlock>Put the form above a <Anchor Href="docs/components/table">Table</Anchor> and give the table the filtered rows: the form keeps the rest of the address, such as the table's sort, and drops page numbers, so filtering goes back to page 1.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="filter-form-books" />
<TextBlock>Page numbers are <Code>page</Code> or a key ending in <Code>-page</Code>, <Code>_page</Code> or <Code>.page</Code>, as a table with a <Code>QueryPrefix</Code> writes (<Code>books-page</Code>). The <Anchor Href="demos/filter-form/order-list">order list demo</Anchor> does the same for a whole page.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Your own inputs</Heading>
<TextBlock>Put any input with a <Code>name</Code> in the form, and list the names in <Code>Fields</Code> so the form doesn't also carry their old values over from the address.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@OwnFields</Code>
<TextBlock>With the script, a text, search, email, phone or URL input of your own applies after a pause in typing, like <Code>FilterSearch</Code>; a select or checkbox applies when it changes; and any other input, such as a number, applies a moment after it changes. Give each one a label, here an <Code>aria-label</Code>, since the form doesn't add one.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Saying how many match</Heading>
<TextBlock>New results aren't announced, so show the count in a live region (<Code>aria-live="polite"</Code>) next to the list, as the examples do: screen readers read it each time it changes.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Sizes and its own words</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Size</Code> on each filter field to match a compact toolbar (the buttons stay their own size), <Code>Label</Code> for the search landmark's name ("Filters"), and <Code>ApplyText</Code> and <Code>ClearText</Code> for the buttons. A search box's and a select's <Code>Label</Code> is hidden but names it (a select requires one; the search defaults to "Search"), a <Code>FilterCheckbox</Code>'s is its required visible text, and a <Code>FilterSelect</Code>'s first option reads "Label: All" unless you set <Code>AllText</Code>.</TextBlock>

@if (OverviewParts.Of(Component, Api).Count > 0)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Parts</Heading>
}
<OverviewParts Component="Component" />

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        <FilterForm Label="Filter customers">
            <FilterSearch Placeholder="Search customers" />
            <FilterSelect Name="status" Label="Status" EnumType="typeof(CustomerStatus)" />
        </FilterForm>

        @code {
            // The page reads the filters from the address and filters its own data.
            [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "q")] private string? Search { get; set; }
            [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "status")] private string? Status { get; set; }
        }
        """;

    private const string OwnFields = """
        <FilterForm Fields="@(["over"])">
            <FilterSearch Placeholder="Search invoices" />
            <input type="number" name="over" value="@Over" min="0" aria-label="Over (USD)" />
        </FilterForm>

        @code {
            [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "over")] private decimal? Over { get; set; }
        }
        """;
}
